"It starts deep underground, where some rock is hot enough to melt. Melted rock is called magma. Magma is lighter than the solid rock around it, so it slowly pushes its way up. On the way it may collect in a large space called a magma chamber.": "b3e9d8cee7fd",
"Like soda, magma has gas dissolved in it, mostly water vapor and carbon dioxide. Deep down, the weight of the rock above keeps the gas in. But as magma rises, the pressure drops, and the gas comes out as bubbles. The higher the bubbles rise, the bigger they grow. The magma swells, and it needs more room.": "da788b1992d7",
"Magma is melted rock under the ground.": "23f88781db83",
"Most volcanoes are found where plates meet, and many stand in a ring around the Pacific Ocean. Taiwan is on that ring. In the north, beside Taipei, is the Tatun Volcanic Group. Steam and gas still rise from the ground there, and scientists think magma may still lie below. Off the coast of Yilan, Guishan Island is a volcano too.": "30a8184fad15",
"Nobody knows when it will erupt again.": "bd503a2de030",
"Red lava ran down the mountain.": "1b5d3122918a",
"Shake a bottle of soda and open it. You know what happens next. Before you opened it, the gas was hidden in the drink, held there by pressure. The moment the pressure was gone, the gas rushed out as bubbles and took the soda with it. A volcano works in much the same way.": "86222e6c9b1a",
"Shake a bottle of soda and open it. You know what happens next. Before you opened it, the gas was hidden in the drink, held there by pressure. The moment the pressure was gone, the gas rushed out as bubbles and took the soda with it. A volcano works in much the same way. It starts deep underground, where some rock is hot enough to melt. Melted rock is called magma. Magma is lighter than the solid rock around it, so it slowly pushes its way up. On the way it may collect in a large space called a magma chamber. Like soda, magma has gas dissolved in it, mostly water vapor and carbon dioxide. Deep down, the weight of the rock above keeps the gas in. But as magma rises, the pressure drops, and the gas comes out as bubbles. The higher the bubbles rise, the bigger they grow. The magma swells, and it needs more room. What happens next depends on the magma. Some magma is runny, a little like syrup. Bubbles slip out of it easily, and the lava flows quietly down the mountain. Other magma is thick and sticky, because it has more of a material called silica in it. Bubbles cannot escape from sticky magma. The pressure builds until the magma is blown apart into ash and broken rock. Most volcanoes are found where plates meet, and many stand in a ring around the Pacific Ocean. Taiwan is on that ring. In the north, beside Taipei, is the Tatun Volcanic Group. Steam and gas still rise from the ground there, and scientists think magma may still lie below. Off the coast of Yilan, Guishan Island is a volcano too. Volcanoes can be dangerous, but they also give us things. Water heated underground comes up as hot springs. Ash breaks down into rich soil. And scientists watch volcanoes closely, measuring small earthquakes, gases, and the swelling of the ground, so that people can be warned in time. A quiet volcano is not always a dead one.": "8184f6005f93",
